@page "/exsheet/xlsx"
@using ExGrid.Docs.Examples.Sheet

<DocsPage Product="ExSheet" Title="Open and save .xlsx"
          Lead="A workbook from Excel opened into an ExSheet, every worksheet a Sheet and every Value computed again, with a report of what the file held that did not come across; and the Workbook saved back as an .xlsx that Excel opens and recalculates.">

    <DocsSection Title="Two packages, and no component">
        <p>
            <code>ExSheet.Xlsx</code> maps a file to a <code>WorkbookDocument</code> and a Workbook to a file; the
            file itself — the zip, its parts, shared strings, styles and cells read as a stream — is
            <code>ExGrid.Data.Xlsx</code>'s. Neither references a component or anything outside .NET, so a server
            converts files with no Blazor in the process, and an application that never touches <code>.xlsx</code>
            carries neither. ExSheet itself references no file format: <b>choosing the file and delivering the
            written one are your application's</b>, as persisting a Sheet Document is. Both are published with the
            rest of the family, at its one version, from <code>0.2.0</code> and its release candidates.
        </p>
        <p>
            In TypeScript, <code>@@ex-grid/data-xlsx</code> (published with 0.3.0) is <code>ExGrid.Data.Xlsx</code>'s
            counterpart: <code>openXlsx</code> reads a <code>Blob</code> or its bytes a batch of cells at a time, and
            <code>createXlsxWriter</code> writes a workbook to a <code>Blob</code>, with the zip inflated and deflated by the
            browser's own <code>DecompressionStream</code> and <code>CompressionStream</code>. It refuses the same files, in the
            same words, and writes the same parts; both run the same cases.
        </p>
        <p>
            Over it, <code>@@ex-grid/sheet-xlsx</code> (published with 0.3.0) is <code>ExSheet.Xlsx</code>'s counterpart:
            <code>openXlsxWorkbook(file, culture)</code> answers the Workbook Document and its Open Report, or refuses the
            file with an <code>XlsxOpenError</code>, and <code>saveXlsxWorkbook(workbook)</code> answers the file as a
            <code>Blob</code>, or refuses the Workbook with an <code>XlsxSaveError</code>. The culture is a
            <code>Culture</code> or its locale, and the options pace, report and cancel through an <code>AbortSignal</code>
            as a read does. It carries, refuses and lists exactly what this package does, in the same words, because both
            run the same cases (<code>tests/cases/sheet-xlsx</code>). ExSheet's React component opens and saves through
            it, as the Example below shows under React.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <b>Reading:</b> <code>XlsxWorkbookReader.ReadAsync(stream, culture)</code> answers the
                <code>WorkbookDocument</code> and its <b>Open Report</b>. Every worksheet becomes a Sheet, in the file's
                order, and the one shown is shown. Numbers, text, booleans, Error Values and Formulas are carried, and
                <b>no stored Value is taken</b>: opening computes every Value again.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>The culture is yours.</b> A workbook stores numbers and dates the same way in every language, so no
                Value read from it depends on a culture. What does is how the user's typing is read afterwards and how
                Values are shown, so you declare it, as you declare a CSV's encoding; it is never guessed from the file.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>Refused by name, and nothing opens</b>: anything that would change a Value or the number a cell
                shows — a Formula the engine cannot read, a defined name, another workbook, an Excel Table, a legacy
                array formula, a Number Format the engine does not read, a file that is not a plain
                <code>.xlsx</code>. The <code>XlsxOpenException</code> names the Sheet, the cell and what it held.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>The Open Report</b> lists what was not carried — merged cells, conditional formatting, notes,
                hyperlinks, frozen panes, font names and sizes and the rest — and the cells whose Formulas name a
                function ExSheet does not support, which open as <code>#NAME?</code>. ExSheet shows nothing of it; the
                Example below lists it. Saving again loses what it lists.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Opening a file in the Sheet">
        <p>
            <code>OpenAsync(read)</code> replaces the Workbook with the document <code>read</code> answers, and takes
            its place in the Sheet's order at the call: call it from the handler that hears the user's choice — an
            <code>InputFile</code>'s change — with the read still to do. The inputs made before it are taken
            against the Workbook they were made on; <b>what is typed while the file is read waits, and lands on the
            Workbook it opens</b>, however long the read takes. A paste aimed at the old cells is refused aloud
            rather than written into the new ones.
        </p>
        <p>
            Under React the same command is <code>open(read)</code> on the component's ref, and <code>read</code> takes an
            <code>AbortSignal</code> in place of the token:
            <code>signal =&gt; openXlsxWorkbook(file, 'en-US', { signal }).then(read =&gt; read.document)</code>. It
            answers a promise of whether the file opened, and a cancelled read rejects with the signal's reason. The grid
            underneath gains the seam it is built on, <code>cancellableInOrder(handling, cancelledTooLate)</code>, beside
            <code>inOrder(handling)</code> for work no Escape cancels, for a command of your own that should take its
            place among the user's inputs. The Sheet's own ref carries <code>inOrder(handling)</code> too, and
            <code>focus()</code>, which hands the keyboard back to the Sheet from a control of yours beside it. As in Blazor, the Sheet's toolbar and tabs wait behind the opening as its cells
            do, so a format chosen from the toolbar while the file is read is made on the Workbook the file opens, and an
            Escape typed on any of them cancels the read.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <b>Refused by name while a cell is being edited</b> (<code>SheetRefusedException</code>, said in the
                Sheet), as a save is, and nothing is read: the opening would throw away what is being typed. The edit
                stays open, and the typing goes on into it.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>While the file is read, the Sheet says so</b> — “Opening a file. Press Escape in the Sheet to
                cancel.” — and its grid is busy, painted as a paste under way is (the <code>--ex-pasting-*</code>
                tokens, <a href="exgrid/appearance">Appearance</a>).
            </li>
            <li>
                <b><kbd>Escape</kbd> in the Sheet cancels it</b> — on its cells, its toolbar or its tabs: an Escape typed
                elsewhere on your page is not the Sheet's. The Sheet's grid shows the opening from the call on, while
                it waits behind earlier inputs and while it reads (its Settled Stamp names it), and a plain Escape
                typed against a page that shows it cancels it and does nothing else: the read's token is cancelled,
                <code>OpenAsync</code> answers <code>false</code>, the Workbook stays as it was, and the Sheet says
                so; an opening cancelled before its turn ends cancelled, even if a cell was opened for editing
                meanwhile. An Escape typed against a page that did not yet show the opening is an ordinary key, however
                soon after the call it arrives: what it was typed against decides it, not when it comes. A second
                Escape finds the opening cancelled and says there was nothing to cancel. An Escape that names an
                opening that has landed changes nothing, and the Sheet says it came too late; one that finds it
                already over otherwise — refused, or cancelled — says there was nothing to cancel. Whether the cancel
                reaches the read before it lands is the one outcome that races, as a paste's does, and each is said.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>A refused read leaves the Workbook as it was</b>, the Sheet says why, and the exception is
                <code>OpenAsync</code>'s, for you to show.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>When the file lands it replaces the whole Workbook</b>, as a different <code>Workbook</code>
                handed over does: the undo stack is cleared, and <code>WorkbookChanged</code> is raised with it. <b>The
                Linked Tables you declared are gone with the Workbook that held them</b>: a file declares none, so
                declare them again on the Workbook opened if it should read them. An exception your handler throws
                then is <code>OpenAsync</code>'s, and the file stays opened.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>Only the Sheet's own inputs wait behind the opening</b>: keys, presses, its toolbar and its tabs.
                A method your application calls while the file is read — <code>UndoAsync</code>, a Linked Table pushed,
                a parameter set — is not an input and is not held, and what it changes is replaced when the file lands.
            </li>
            <li>
                Setting <code>Workbook</code> or <code>Document</code> keeps working, for a document you already hold.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Saving">
        <p>
            <code>SaveAsync(write)</code> lends <code>write</code> the Workbook as the inputs made before the call
            left it, at its own place in the order: hand it to <code>XlsxWorkbookWriter.WriteAsync</code>, which takes
            its snapshot before it first yields and builds the file from that snapshot a slice at a time, yielding
            between slices as a read does, with progress (<code>XlsxSaveOptions.Progress</code>) and cancellation.
            The inputs made after the save do not wait for the file to be written, and do not reach it. The progress
            is told on whatever thread the write is running on: show it through a <code>Progress&lt;T&gt;</code> made
            on the component, or <code>InvokeAsync</code>.
            Every Sheet is written with its Cell Formats and widths, and <b>every Formula with the Value on screen</b>,
            <code>RAND()</code>'s included, so a program that does not calculate still reads what the user saw; the
            workbook is marked to be calculated in full when Excel opens it.
        </p>
        <p>
            Under React it is <code>save(write)</code> on the ref, handed to <code>saveXlsxWorkbook</code>, which takes its
            snapshot before it first yields and answers the file as a <code>Blob</code>:
            <code>save(async workbook =&gt; { file = await saveXlsxWorkbook(workbook); })</code>.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <b>Refused by name while a cell is being edited</b> (<code>SheetRefusedException</code>): the file would
                quietly lack what is being typed. Commit or cancel the edit, then save.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>Refused by name, with nothing written</b> (<code>XlsxSaveException</code>): a cell that shows
                <code>#CIRC!</code>, a Formula Excel would not take, text longer than a cell holds, and more than the
                limits <code>XlsxSaveOptions</code> sets. <code>MaxCells</code> counts every element the file
                writes: the cells, and each row a row format covers, so a row format over every row writes a
                million of them.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>Each Linked Table is saved as an Excel Table</b>, on a worksheet of its own name after the Sheets,
                holding its rows as they stand, and a Formula that reads it is written as it is
                (<code>=SUM(Positions[PV])</code>), so it keeps working in Excel. <b>Such a file opens in Excel, and not
                in ExSheet</b>: nothing in a file can say that a table is your application's data, so the worksheet is
                not read back as a Linked Table, and a Formula that reads an Excel Table is refused on opening until
                ExSheet reads Excel Tables. A Linked Table that has not answered (<code>#GETTING_DATA</code>), or whose
                name is already a Sheet's, refuses the save.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Example">
        <p>
            Open an <code>.xlsx</code> of your own, or save this claim and open what you saved. The Example keeps the
            saved file in a link you click to download, since a WebAssembly page cannot start a download by itself
            without a script. The link carries the file in its address, and a browser takes at most 2 MB there
            (2,097,152 characters, about 1.5 MB of file): a larger saved file is said and given no link, never cut
            short. A file over 10,000,000 bytes is refused as it is read. While a large file is read, type into the
            Sheet, or press <kbd>Escape</kbd> in it.
        </p>
        <p>
            Under React the saved file is a <code>Blob</code>, and its link an object URL, so no size of file is too
            large for it; a file larger than the read's limits is refused by name as it is read.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(XlsxOpenSave)" Mud="typeof(XlsxOpenSaveMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Sheet/Expenses.cs"])" />
    </DocsSection>

